            "type": "output_text",
            "annotations": [],
            "logprobs": [],
            "text": "{\"claims\":[{\"type\":\"domain\",\"location\":\"Icc (0 : ℝ) 1\",\"explanation\":\"The closed interval counts both endpoints of a period. For N = 1 and f(t) = sin(2πt), the zeroth derivative has zeros at 0, 1/2, and 1, while the first derivative has zeros at 1/4 and 3/4. Counting multiplicities therefore gives M 0 = 3 and M 1 = 2, contradicting the asserted monotonicity.\",\"prediction\":\"A counterexample to the original proposition should be obtainable by defining mult as the least order of a nonzero derivative when one exists, and M by the stated sum.\",\"probe_kind\":\"statement\",\"comparison\":null,\"witnesses\":null,\"edit_from\":null,\"edit_to\":null,\"bridge\":\"instance\",\"obligation\":\"Construct mult and M satisfying their hypotheses in Lean, and check the endpoint counts. The informal problem does not specify a counting interval; interpreting its finite count as zeros in one period without double-counting the shared endpoint remains a translation question.\"}],\"summary\":\"Counting zeros on [0,1] appears to double-count a periodic endpoint, making the formal monotonicity claim false even for a single sine term.\"}"
          }
        ],
